// Words: Jonathan's canonical commentary txt. Structure: his Notion pages.
import type { V205BookData } from '../types';

export const DATA: V205BookData = {
  book: "James",
  display: "James",
  chapters: {
    "1": {"chapter":1,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"In line with the other epistles, this formerly fits in with the same theme as 1, 2 Peter, 1, 2, and 3 John, and Jude. James’s topic doesn’t spread too far from the topic that Peter sticks to on the great ministry of suffering. There’s nothing more pleasant in a Christian’s life than to suffer for living for Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"We don’t often see trials and troubles as pleasant, and I think that is our first problem. The fact that God has allowed trials in our life is a sure sign that He is working on us, and it’s a sign of our faith. This does not mean we should look for trouble. This simply means that if we have no trouble, we probably have no faith. The fact is, when we place our faith in God, we are ultimately saying “God, allow me trouble. Help me to grow.” How do you grow? Through trials. You can grow through study, reading, and books, but that growth will only be head knowledge and it will take years to learn. God wants us to learn quickly, and the quickest way to learn is through hardships."},{"kind":"para","text":"As we go through this short letter/small book of the Bible, may we open our eyes and hearts to the ministry of suffering and be prepared with the same mind as Christ that no matter what we might face, we will continue to be faithful to the Lord."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":12,"title":"Testing Faith By Trials","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We understand that the author of this book is obviously James. The writer is questioned quite often throughout history, and no one knows for sure which James wrote this book. It could be a total of four different Jameses. The evidence very heavily points to Jesus’ half-brother James."},{"kind":"para","text":"It couldn’t be John’s brother James (he was beheaded before he had the chance to write this letter if that were the case). It couldn’t be James the Less, for there isn’t much known about him concerning his mentorship, let alone his church authority (in which this James is writing with utmost authority). There’s another James mentioned, but not really in regards to his ministry for Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"That only leaves Jesus’ half-brother, who took up the mantle of the church in Jerusalem right after John’s brother “James” was beheaded. The interesting part about this is not that James is writing it. The interesting part is that he never mentions that he is the half-brother of Jesus at all. He quickly dismisses his authority right off the bat. He simply calls himself “a servant.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"More importantly, in the Greek, that word means “a bond slave.” He doesn’t want the topic to be about him. He has a very pressing and important issue to stress on. He says he is writing this to the twelve tribes, indicating he is writing to the Christian Jews during this time. This, of course, is during a time of great persecution against the church."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter had almost died, John’s brother was beheaded, and the governmental authorities were burning down churches and cities because there were people confessing Christ. In fact, Peter was jailed around this time of the writing and stuck in prison for quite some time. James would be martyred around the same time as Peter. It’s not looking very great for strong characters of the faith, and that is why James has chosen to write this… 2 My brethren, count it all joy when ye fall into divers temptations;"},{"kind":"para","text":"He cuts to the chase. What was the most important matter to press upon in this letter? “Divers temptations.” This means “trials of all sorts.” James knows there are trials in the church. He knows the oppression of the governments and the mocking and scorning of the people. He knows that there are pinpoint specific examples he could be giving to illustrate the importance here. But he also knows that people will find new and (if I may say) “exciting” ways to humiliate, persecute, torture, execute, and demonize Christians."},{"kind":"para","text":"I still find it fascinating that there is so much hate for Christians today. It hasn’t stopped with James. We have become far more spoiled here, but it wouldn’t take much even in America to start an uprising against Christian culture. We are the least accepted people in the land today, which is funny because they blame us for being less accepting. I believe everyone should believe what they want, but that doesn’t mean they are right. I cannot stop you from believing a lie. I can only tell you the truth. The truth, however, makes enemies of the world, because the world hates truth. 3 Knowing this, that the trying of your faith worketh patience."},{"kind":"para","text":"Peter says the trying of our faith makes us stronger. He compares it to “gold being refined.” James, on the other hand, suggests that our faith being tried gives us patience. I can understand trials providing strength because they help us learn how to navigate better and build up stronger for the truth. But patience? The last thing I want to do in a trial is wait. I think that’s the last thing anyone wants to do."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, what James is saying is that the trying of our faith should build our patience. Every time we are tried or suffer through an issue, we should learn how to wait a little better. The first time we go through a trial, God makes us wait through it. The second time, we come to trust the Lord a little more (and yes, there will be a second time, and no, it won’t be the last). The third time, we realize that God is working through the trial. The fourth time, we become a little more lenient on the Lord, knowing He will provide. The fifth time and beyond, we have a strong faith and can have peace through a storm."},{"kind":"para","text":"Patience allows us to reflect and lean into the knowledge that God is working something out, even if we never see what it is. God does not waste a trial, and neither should you. Use trials to grow in patience. How? Well, James answers this."},{"kind":"para","text":"4 But let patience have her perfect work, that ye may be perfect and entire, wanting nothing."},{"kind":"para","text":"Trials must work patience in you, and once you have come to terms with the fact that God can do whatever He wants with His children for His (and our) ultimate good pleasure, we become more whole. This is a great example to lead us back to David."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The LORD is my shepherd; I shall not want. He maketh me to lie down in green pastures: he leadeth me beside the still waters. He restoreth my soul: he leadeth me in the paths of righteousness for his name's sake. Yea, though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil: for thou art with me; thy rod and thy staff they comfort me. Thou preparest a table before me in the presence of mine enemies: thou anointest my head with oil; my cup runneth over. Surely goodness and mercy shall follow me all the days of my life: and I will dwell in the house of the LORD for ever.","ref":"Psalm 23:1-6"},{"kind":"para","text":"David could only write this during a time of immense trouble. He was at peace with God in the middle of a war with people he loved. The valley of death was around him, yet he didn’t fear. He had God’s patience. His life was not his own. That’s the reminder we should take here. We do not own our lives anymore. When you are attacked in a conflict or trial, remember your worth is in the Lord Jesus Christ. An attack on you is an attack on Him, and God does not take kindly to that. Let tribulation work patience in you!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And not only so, but we glory in tribulations also: knowing that tribulation worketh patience; And patience, experience; and experience, hope: And hope maketh not ashamed; because the love of God is shed abroad in our hearts by the Holy Ghost which is given unto us.","ref":"Romans 5:3-5"},{"kind":"para","text":"So you ask, “How can we learn patience?” Through trouble. “How can we grow for the Lord and have more hope and peace?” Through trouble. “How can we be comforted in this life of trials and torment?” Through trouble. Trouble is an indicator of a growing Christian. A troubled Christian is a growing Christian. 5 If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of God, that giveth to all men liberally, and upbraideth not; and it shall be given him."},{"kind":"para","text":"Here’s one “How should I become wiser in my Christian life?” Through trouble. When trouble arises, we tend to lean on God more and depend upon His strength and provision. If we do not do this, we will keep the trouble for ourselves, and it will not go away. You want to grow? You have to lean on God and pray for wisdom. That is, I believe, how Solomon became known as one of the wisest people to have ever lived. Not through a wish that was granted by his prayer, but through trouble in his life, the Lord allowing him to learn hard lessons fast. Great wisdom is given to people who have grown in trials. No trials, no wisdom. 6 But let him ask in faith, nothing wavering. For he that wavereth is like a wave of the sea driven with the wind and tossed."},{"kind":"para","text":"James is really accurate with this portrait here. Many of our prayers do not get answered because we don’t really mean what we pray for. We pray, but we pray in a fleshly sense. We pray for our troubles to go away. Rather, we should be praying that the Lord would bring us wisdom through the trouble. We should pray that God would make Himself known and that we can do nothing but glorify His name in the midst of a storm. Otherwise, the storm will take us overboard, and our prayers will just be like the storm we are stuck in (reckless and hopeless). 7 For let not that man think that he shall receive any thing of the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"Someone with no faith and no patience through trials is someone who has no prayer. They may openly pray, and they may be good at faking it, but they don’t really trust the Lord in their prayer. These people are ones that have it in their mind that they will just take care of the problems themselves rather than trust in some being they can’t even hear personally… They are ones that say that God does not speak. He doesn’t speak to them because they don’t listen to His Word. 8 A double minded man is unstable in all his ways."},{"kind":"para","text":"A double-minded person is someone who cannot make up their minds about the Lord yet. They claim to trust Him, yet they don’t live a life that exemplifies that. They claim to know Him, yet they don’t talk about Him. They claim to love Him, yet they aren’t spending time with Him. They claim to have faith, yet they are always upset and annoyed by the world around them. They are double-minded. They have a mind on the Lord and on themselves, and when we are more in tune with our flesh, it makes it very hard to hear the Lord’s guidance for us. 9 Let the brother of low degree rejoice in that he is exalted: 10 But the rich, in that he is made low: because as the flower of the grass he shall pass away. 11 For the sun is no sooner risen with a burning heat, but it withereth the grass, and the flower thereof falleth, and the grace of the fashion of it perisheth: so also shall the rich man fade away in his ways."},{"kind":"para","text":"Let’s take money out of the equation for a second. Richness can never be measured by wealthiness. Richness is measured by closeness. Whatever you might be closest to. Someone who is rich in the world usually doesn’t have any eternal or lasting significance. They don’t usually have a legacy or lifestyle to pass down to the next generation."},{"kind":"para","text":"I’ve seen many astrologers and self-proclaimed “scientists” who have far better knowledge about the happenings of space than they do about the happenings of Earth. Their legacy to pass down to the next generation will be on the amazement of space. Yet, how will the knowledge of space get the next generation to stand up against tyranny? How will it get the next generation to love each other more? How will it get the next generation to think past themselves, and build upon something greater than themselves?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Space is great, and I have no quarrels against it, but until we are able to take care of where we are now, space is really a meaningless venture to pass down to the next generation. We are so busy getting off this planet as quickly as possible that we have altogether neglected our care for others and thankfulness of where we currently are. Their works (as this verse says) will be burned up, and will be left to nothing because they have put their richness in material wealth, rather than in future (i.e. eternal) importance. 12 Blessed is the man that endureth temptation: for when he is tried, he shall receive the crown of life, which the Lord hath promised to them that love him."},{"kind":"para","text":"If we can endure (that is to sit through and wait) in trials, we will receive life which God has promised to us. God has made us promises, and life is one of them. This only comes about if we are able to grow in patience. Spurgeon said, “He that has love to God finds joy in that love. He also finds blessedness in suffering for that love.”"}]}]},{"start":13,"end":21,"title":"God Won't Use Evil Means to Test","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God does not put sin in our lives to test us. He never baits us into disobedience. When God allows trials, He allows them through a scope (or lens) of building upon righteousness and humility. He wants us to grow in several areas. Patience is the first stop that James mentions."},{"kind":"para","text":"We can only grow through patience if we are enduring the trials with the correct lens. God’s lens is not focused on sin. His lens is focused on righteousness. Our lens focuses on sin, and then we try to accuse God of allowing sin in our lives (or the sin of betrayal from others) thinking that He is trying to tempt us into evil with evil. Not so."},{"kind":"para","text":"He doesn’t tempt you with anything. He works on you. He wants you to transform. He does not want you to stay bent out of shape, but rather He desires to shape you into something far more useful than what you are today. 14 But every man is tempted, when he is drawn away of his own lust, and enticed."},{"kind":"para","text":"Blaming God for being drawn away from Him, whether through your own sin or someone else’s, is a deceit to yourself. You cannot even blame Satan for this deceit because you, as a child of God who now knows your choice, chose to go against the Lord and fulfill your own lust. Lust simply means anything that you desire more than what you should. Believe it or not, many “lust” after their own flesh. They “lust” after their anger and bitterness. They “lust” after their disconnection from others. They “lust” after their hurts and pains. They have tricked their own brain into thinking that it is Satan drawing them into this, when really it’s just their own lusts. They don’t allow God’s patience to grow them, but they just accept their trials as something that was bound to come because God always does this. That is not true."},{"kind":"para","text":"You are either going to be molded into what God is desiring, or you are going to reject God’s transformative trials. If you choose to reject it, you cannot place blame on the Lord, nor on Satan. That is entirely on you. 15 Then when lust hath conceived, it bringeth forth sin: and sin, when it is finished, bringeth forth death."},{"kind":"para","text":"Lust, no matter the kind, brings sin, and sin brings death. If you want to “live life to its fullest,” you must learn to reject the lust of the flesh. D.L. Moody said, “Sin is like a river, which begins in a quiet spring and ends in a stormy sea.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Reject the standard thoughts you have towards trials and God. Reject your mindset of bitterness and anger. Reject envy and hypocrisy. Hold to the Lord and allow Him to shape you. It will take time, and it will hurt in many areas, but the worst thing you can do is reject the Lord’s shaping of you, because you then become useless to Him. 16 Do not err, my beloved brethren. 17 Every good gift and every perfect gift is from above, and cometh down from the Father of lights, with whom is no variableness, neither shadow of turning."},{"kind":"para","text":"What are the good gifts? Anything God can give you to shape you. If we receive God’s trials with gladness, they become “good gifts” to us. We understand that suffering is a gift (I hope) at this point. It has been put this way: “A trial is a sacred trust from God.” God “entrusts” us with a trial, and that should not be taken lightly. 18 Of his own will begat he us with the word of truth, that we should be a kind of firstfruits of his creatures. 19 Wherefore, my beloved brethren, let every man be swift to hear, slow to speak, slow to wrath:"},{"kind":"para","text":"Why is this important? Because we are examples. If we fail in our suffering, we will be an example. If we succeed in our suffering, we will be an example. We are God’s first fruits to someone around us. If we experience trials before anyone else does, God is entrusting us to be the right example in those trials. He knows, of course, how we will complete those trials (or not complete those trials). But he is giving us the opportunity to minister."},{"kind":"para","text":"The ultimate example of ministry is how you work through trials. The best way to face trials is through growth in patience, which causes us to become “swift to hear, slow to speak, and slow to wrath.” If we are slow of hearing, quick in talking, and angry and bitter rapidly, we are not growing in our trial that the Lord has placed in our lives. In fact, we are squandering the trial. 20 For the wrath of man worketh not the righteousness of God. 21 Wherefore lay apart all filthiness and superfluity of naughtiness, and receive with meekness the engrafted word, which is able to save your souls."},{"kind":"para","text":"Receiving God’s Word is only important to those who believe in God’s Word. This might be hard to hear, but I could argue that if we do not receive God’s Word (whether through preaching or personal study), we do not actually believe God’s Word. If we do not believe God’s Word, we cannot put away these sinful notions of fleshly desires (or filthiness) and an overflowing amount of wickedness (or superfluity of naughtiness)."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":27,"title":"God Uses His Own Word to Test","entries":[{"start":22,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here’s the essence of the message: If we are not willing to act upon what we’ve heard from God’s Word (not man’s Word, but aligning with God’s Word), then we are listening in vain. In fact, if we don’t do what we are told through God’s Word, we immediately begin to forget what we are supposed to do. We become self-focused and end up back in our former state, which is even worse than the trial we were currently in."},{"kind":"para","text":"I’d rather be through a trial with God’s help than to live through the world in my own strength and pleasure. Why? Because we “shall be blessed in” our “deed.” Whatever we touch should be soaked with dependency on the Lord, so that even the trials look like peace in our lives."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The difficulty arises when people believe they are compelled to endure trials, look to the Lord, and minister solely because it is their religious duty. However, aside from what James is about to say regarding what true religion is, if we are not willing to control our own mouths and keep our hearts focused on the Lord, we are merely hypocritically pretending in our religion."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have observed many “religionists” who would disagree with James here. They attend church “most” Sundays, have memorized various types of prayers, and wear fancy suits and dresses to fit the part. Their entire family accompanies them to services, and they have mastered the traditions and penance to blend in with the crowd. Yet, at home, every other word they utter is foul. Their hearts are set on alcohol and lust, they seek wrath and anger, and they revel in filthiness and a superfluity of naughtiness without concern."},{"kind":"para","text":"They are not doers of the word, and I am not even sure if they are actually hearing the word (assuming the word is even given in their churches). They are vain religionists. James has a word for you here. 27 Pure religion and undefiled before God and the Father is this, To visit the fatherless and widows in their affliction, and to keep himself unspotted from the world."},{"kind":"para","text":"Many religionists don’t even do this successfully. They neglect the fatherless and widows. They aren’t religious because if they were, they would be visiting the widows and helping the orphans with every fiber of their being. God, however, is not looking for religious folk. He’s looking for committed and obedient folk."},{"kind":"para","text":"He’s looking for people willing to stand during the time of trial and continue forward with joy and longing for the Lord to show Himself in the lives of others. He’s not looking for those who forget His Word. He’s looking for those who reflect His Word. And what better time to reflect Christ than through the most difficult times of our lives?"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":22,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":26}],"verses":{"1":"James, a servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ, to the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad, greeting.","13":"Let no man say when he is tempted, I am tempted of God: for God cannot be tempted with evil, neither tempteth he any man:","22":"But be ye doers of the word, and not hearers only, deceiving your own selves.","23":"For if any be a hearer of the word, and not a doer, he is like unto a man beholding his natural face in a glass:","24":"For he beholdeth himself, and goeth his way, and straightway forgetteth what manner of man he was.","25":"But whoso looketh into the perfect law of liberty, and continueth therein, he being not a forgetful hearer, but a doer of the work, this man shall be blessed in his deed.","26":"If any man among you seem to be religious, and bridleth not his tongue, but deceiveth his own heart, this man's religion is vain."}},
    "2": {"chapter":2,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 2"},{"kind":"para","text":"Group: Saturday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: James (the half-brother of Jesus) opened this letter up by displaying the necessity of experiencing trials. He talks about how faith is proven and tested, and it’s not through how much knowledge you have in the Bible, but rather how faithful you remain through intense difficulties. A person is only known to be genuine if they stay on fire for God through God-given difficulties. He also made it clear that God doesn’t use evil to give tests to His children, but will allow certain trials to come through a means of escape (in other words, God’s Will will always be an option when we go through a trial. We don’t have to use our will, or that of Satan’s). Every trial and test that we go through in this life will be driven by God’s Will and Word, and if we are going through a trial, it is because that is BIBLICALLY appropriate. Trials are God-given. God entrusts US with the trials and we should not falter or squander the opportunities God gives us through them."},{"kind":"para","text":"This chapter will continue on that thread and give a little bit of a deeper look at what God may use to grant us trials and times of testing in our own lives as followers of Christ."}],"previously":["James (the half-brother of Jesus) opened this letter up by displaying the necessity of experiencing trials. He talks about how faith is proven and tested, and it’s not through how much knowledge you have in the Bible, but rather how faithful you remain through intense difficulties. He also made it clear that God doesn’t use evil to give tests to His children, but will allow certain trials to come through a means of escape (in other words, God’s Will will always be an option when we go through a trial. We don’t have to use our will, or that of Satan’s). Every trial and test that we go through in this life will be driven by God’s Will and Word, and if we are going through a trial, it is because that is BIBLICALLY appropriate. Trials are God-given. God entrusts US with the trials and we should not falter or squander the opportunities God gives us through them. This chapter will continue on that thread and give a little bit of a deeper look at what God may use to grant us trials and times of testing in our own lives as followers of Christ."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":13,"title":"Tests by Attitude","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The fabric of Christian ability is not based on who we think deserves our grace and attention versus who we think doesn’t. We cannot treat anyone differently based on mental capacity, social status, or wealth. We must not respect people because we think they deserve our respect. We must be available to be a person of use and a Christlike example to all."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is hard for us to do, because there are some people who just downright annoy us. They are vain and evil people. Some are self-centered and unwilling to listen. James (as well as many other biblical authors) makes it clear that we are not to treat people as unworthy of our Christian conduct."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For there is no respect of persons with God.","ref":"Romans 2:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"This was foreign to those in that time. The Jews were sanctioned and separated by personal traditions and practices in themselves, let alone the Gentiles, and those that were of completely differing viewpoints."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then Peter opened his mouth, and said, Of a truth I perceive that God is no respecter of persons:","ref":"Acts 10:34"},{"kind":"para","text":"The disciples were some of the first to learn that we are not to treat other people’s beliefs as inferior to ours, but rather just give them the truth. We don’t have to attack their faith, but we should be willing to stand up for our own. The best way we can do that is to treat every person of every background the same as you’d treat a friend. In the end, those who are genuine and those who are not will be found out anyway. 2 For if there come unto your assembly a man with a gold ring, in goodly apparel, and there come in also a poor man in vile raiment; 3 And ye have respect to him that weareth the gay clothing, and say unto him, Sit thou here in a good place; and say to the poor, Stand thou there, or sit here under my footstool: 4 Are ye not then partial in yourselves, and are become judges of evil thoughts?"},{"kind":"para","text":"James addresses the differences that people make between someone with wealth and someone without any money at all. We live in an age where wealthy people are noticed and known. If a famous actor came into our church today, everyone would be making it a point to gather around that person and make them feel welcome. However, if a homeless man came in, we would probably keep away as much as possible or tell the pastor about this person that doesn’t really seem to belong."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a sad take, because it was obviously common back then, and nothing has changed today. James is preaching against that mindset. He doesn’t want people to see others as status, but rather see them as souls. Money, or no money, it doesn’t matter. Every person in the world is destined for the grave and for one eternal home. One of rest, one of suffering. We have the chance to help them to rest. 5 Hearken, my beloved brethren, Hath not God chosen the poor of this world rich in faith, and heirs of the kingdom which he hath promised to them that love him?"},{"kind":"para","text":"We are borderline poor. We don’t have money to buy the nicest clothes, the fanciest jewelry, or the latest and greatest gadgets or cars. We can barely afford a rent payment and groceries. But our faith is far more valuable, and it is only by our faith that we are surviving. Why? Because God provides for the faithful."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But seek ye first the kingdom of God, and his righteousness; and all these things shall be added unto you.","ref":"Matthew 6:33"},{"kind":"para","text":"We should be worse off, in all honesty, but God does not see us that way. Our faith, to God, has more value than money."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But without faith it is impossible to please him: for he that cometh to God must believe that he is, and that he is a rewarder of them that diligently seek him.","ref":"Hebrews 11:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"6 But ye have despised the poor. Do not rich men oppress you, and draw you before the judgment seats? 7 Do not they blaspheme that worthy name by the which ye are called?"},{"kind":"para","text":"The rich are just as much sinners as the poor. In fact, I could argue they are bigger sinners. For they have power and money behind their sins. The poor do not. 8 If ye fulfil the royal law according to the scripture, Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself, ye do well: 9 But if ye have respect to persons, ye commit sin, and are convinced of the law as transgressors."},{"kind":"para","text":"Religions live by laws. James (following the Words of Christ) is giving us two laws, which are pretty straightforward."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thy heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy mind, and with all thy strength: this is the first commandment. And the second is like, namely this, Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself. There is none other commandment greater than these.","ref":"Mark 12:30-31"},{"kind":"para","text":"The truth is, if we are not loving our neighbors as ourselves, we are not keeping God’s first law (which is in loving Him with everything we have). James says, there are two laws you are likely to follow. That’s either the law of love to those around you, or the law of respecting persons (also known as the “law of transgressors”). The law of transgressors goes against the law of God. It goes against our love for the Lord and our faith practices. If we live by the law of transgressors, we cannot live by the law of love. 10 For whosoever shall keep the whole law, and yet offend in one point, he is guilty of all."},{"kind":"para","text":"A vital and important verse to memorize and highlight. Breaking one commandment makes you guilty, period. You break one law today (no matter what the law is), you are still guilty of breaking the law, whether it was seriously bad or not. The truth is, we are all breakers of God’s law, and James lets it be known how guilty we truly are. D.L. Moody best illustrates this by saying, “We divide up sin into sins and miss the gravity. God gave one law, consisting of Ten Commandments.” 11 For he that said, Do not commit adultery, said also, Do not kill. Now if thou commit no adultery, yet if thou kill, thou art become a transgressor of the law. 12 So speak ye, and so do, as they that shall be judged by the law of liberty."},{"kind":"para","text":"James makes it clear that there is no room for a person to say, “Well, I am a good person. I have not broken the more serious laws. I’ve never committed adultery or killed.” No, you have actually."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Whosoever hateth his brother is a murderer: and ye know that no murderer hath eternal life abiding in him.","ref":"1 John 3:15"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But I say unto you, That whosoever looketh on a woman to lust after her hath committed adultery with her already in his heart.","ref":"Matthew 5:28"},{"kind":"para","text":"There’s no excuse. But even if you have never hated someone before, you are still guilty of looking with lust. Or maybe you’ve never done either one of those. You are still guilty of lying. Never lied? You are guilty of stealing. Never stolen? You are still guilty of coveting. You will always be found guilty in God’s law, because that’s what His law is for: to find us guilty so we have no choice but to rely on His mercy. 13 For he shall have judgment without mercy, that hath shewed no mercy; and mercy rejoiceth against judgment."},{"kind":"para","text":"No mercy means greater judgement. God is a God that enjoys mercy. He likes to give His creation mercy (which is why the end has not yet come, though it’s ripe and time for it to have already)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let us therefore come boldly unto the throne of grace, that we may obtain mercy, and find grace to help in time of need.","ref":"Hebrews 4:16"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But God, who is rich in mercy, for his great love wherewith he loved us,","ref":"Ephesians 2:4"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For thou, Lord, art good, and ready to forgive; and plenteous in mercy unto all them that call upon thee.","ref":"Psalm 86:5"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"(For the LORD thy God is a merciful God;) he will not forsake thee, neither destroy thee, nor forget the covenant of thy fathers which he sware unto them.","ref":"Deuteronomy 4:31"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Be ye therefore merciful, as your Father also is merciful.","ref":"Luke 6:36"},{"kind":"para","text":"Mercy rejoices against judgement, because we understand that we deserve our just judgment, but God’s mercy keeps us from getting what we deserve."}]}]},{"start":14,"end":26,"title":"Tests by Works","entries":[{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"May it be well understood that it is not the law that saves us. James has been a great testimony against that idea. This term for “works” is not speaking about the law. The law, as we’ve already mentioned (and as James has made abundantly clear), only makes us guilty before God. If James were speaking about the works of the law here, we would not be saved by works, because it (again) only makes us guilty."},{"kind":"para","text":"James is speaking about a desire to work because of our extended mercy from the Lord. Remember that mercy rejoices over judgement. When we are living in the mercy of God, we have rejoicing work to do. James clarifies… 15 If a brother or sister be naked, and destitute of daily food, 16 And one of you say unto them, Depart in peace, be ye warmed and filled; notwithstanding ye give them not those things which are needful to the body; what doth it profit?"},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s one thing to say, “I’m a Christian, I believe in Christ and His atonement.” And then never show up to church, never serve, and never read your Bible, and a very different thing to not say anything and just serve the Lord out of a glad heart. I can talk the talk, but never walk the walk. Your walk should talk what your talk talks, so that you can be seen walking the walk and talking the talk."},{"kind":"para","text":"The “do as I say, not as I do” mentality doesn’t work in the Christian life. It’s simple, quite frankly. Do as I do. Period. Then you don’t even need to say anything about your faith unless asked. 17 Even so faith, if it hath not works, is dead, being alone."},{"kind":"para","text":"This verse should never be isolated. That would be wrongly dividing the word of truth if we do not include verse 18. 18 Yea, a man may say, Thou hast faith, and I have works: shew me thy faith without thy works, and I will shew thee my faith by my works."},{"kind":"para","text":"Talk is cheap. James makes it a great picture here that you cannot have faith without works. You may have works without faith, but your works would be in vain. Your works by your faith are what ultimately matter here. Not in your holy living, but in your pleasure to serve the Lord with or without eyes on you. We are to be lights in the world of darkness, and the only way to do that is by serving the Lord without having to say a single word about our faith. 19 Thou believest that there is one God; thou doest well: the devils also believe, and tremble."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s not enough to believe in God. It must be a change of life and mind. Our faith must make all things new; otherwise, we will be in the same position as the trembling devils. Demons can say, “I believe in God.” They will not serve God, though…"},{"kind":"para","text":"20 But wilt thou know, O vain man, that faith without works is dead?"},{"kind":"para","text":"A fruit tree that never bears fruit or leaves is useless. What would this dead-looking tree be good for? It won’t provide shade, and it can’t provide sustenance. It’s still a tree, sure, but it’s a phony fruit tree. We, as Christians (without works), are as dead as a fruitless fruit tree. 21 Was not Abraham our father justified by works, when he had offered Isaac his son upon the altar? 22 Seest thou how faith wrought with his works, and by works was faith made perfect? 23 And the scripture was fulfilled which saith, Abraham believed God, and it was imputed unto him for righteousness: and he was called the Friend of God. 24 Ye see then how that by works a man is justified, and not by faith only."},{"kind":"para","text":"These verses get so twisted so much because people are grasping at straws and twisting the words of James here. Let’s look at what is truly said about Abraham."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For what saith the scripture? Abraham believed God, and it was counted unto him for righteousness.","ref":"Romans 4:3"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"By faith Abraham, when he was called to go out into a place which he should after receive for an inheritance, obeyed; and he went out, not knowing whither he went.","ref":"Hebrews 11:8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he believed in the LORD; and he counted it to him for righteousness.","ref":"Genesis 15:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"We see through several areas of scripture (and there’s still more I could mention) that it was by Abraham’s faith alone that counted as righteousness. No works were needed. That is what James is getting at here. But Abraham’s work allowed proof of his faith. Let’s break this sequence down a little further."},{"kind":"para","text":"James describes the work of Abraham offering up his son Isaac. If Abraham is justified by this work, then it was his works that saved him. But anyone who has ever read the story of Abraham knows that Abraham never actually sacrificed his son. If it be the case that Abraham’s work saves him, he squandered it by not killing his son. However, Abraham believed in God, and it was imputed unto him for righteousness."},{"kind":"para","text":"Simply put, Abraham believed in God, believed in what God promised, trusted in God’s promises, and followed God’s promises by faith alone. You demonstrate your faith by your actions. 25 Likewise also was not Rahab the harlot justified by works, when she had received the messengers, and had sent them out another way?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Rahab put her life on the line for these people she didn’t even know. She had faith in the God of these people. It wasn’t just about believing, though. She said, “I’m going to do something about this. I want to be a part of this because I believe God is going to give this land to the Israelites.” 26 For as the body without the spirit is dead, so faith without works is dead also."},{"kind":"para","text":"The summation, faith MUST have works. Otherwise, it’s not really faith. It’s to say something like Melody (my daughter) would say, “I might like to eat broccoli, and I might like the taste of it. I believe you are telling me the truth about how good it is, but I will not try it just because I don’t really want to.” This means she doesn’t really believe what I say about broccoli. Her trying it and liking it would prove that she believes me. There are actions that must be taken if faith is to be at all evident."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":14,"end":14}],"verses":{"1":"My brethren, have not the faith of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Lord of glory, with respect of persons.","14":"What doth it profit, my brethren, though a man say he hath faith, and have not works? can faith save him?"}},
    "3": {"chapter":3,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 3"},{"kind":"para","text":"Group: Saturday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: We have been learning about the different tests that God gives to His children to increase our faith and to affirm/confirm our calling and election. In the last chapter, James points out tests by attitude, and works."},{"kind":"para","text":"Today we will see another test that God uses in the lives of Christians, and the affects it will have on the world around us as well as for eternity."}],"previously":["We have been learning about different tests that God gives to His children to increase our faith and to affirm/confirm our calling and election. In the last chapter, James points out tests by attitude and works. In this chapter, we will see tests through our speech that God uses in the lives of Christians, and the effects it will have on the world around us as well as for eternity."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":18,"title":"Tests By Speech","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In this context, “masters” means “teachers.” When we are teaching, we have to take special care about it. Preachers are very cautious about what they preach, but they have a slight amount of leeway. Teachers have no grace, for if they teach wrong, the perspective of others will be altered, and people will begin to not trust the teacher."},{"kind":"para","text":"It is quite easy to lead people astray in their teaching. Teachers are valuable and very hard to come by. Sadly, there are a lot of people who easily fall for bad teachings and isms. They are enticed by flattering words and superficial speeches with no proof needed to back it up. They just like what they hear, whether it’s true or not, and they stick to it. This is a dangerous ploy. It’s a dangerous thing to openly believe everything everyone else says. Be sure to ask for the facts. We should not believe proof-less (or fruitless) faith."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The language can get lost in translation here. If we misconstrue the Greek, we might assume that James is saying we are accustomed to being offensive to others. This is not the product of his speech here. What he means by “offend” is to “stumble.” Having a background idea of Greek helps cultivate understanding a little better (something I don’t have, so I rely on concordances), but to surmise the message, James wants it to be understood that we all stumble in multiple ways. He continues to talk about this stumbling in a perfect man."},{"kind":"para","text":"He says, “If there are people who don’t stumble and are perfect, then he should be able to have a good stature.” Perfect means “complete” or “fully-grown.” Perfect doesn’t mean “without flaw.” There is no one “perfect” (save Christ, of course), but there are many who are “complete.” 3 Behold, we put bits in the horses' mouths, that they may obey us; and we turn about their whole body."},{"kind":"para","text":"I love the simplicity that James uses here. It’s the same application that can be used today. In order to train a horse and get them to go in the direction you want, you have to reward the horse with “bits.” They don’t know where they are going; they just want the treats so they keep going after it even if you led them to a cliff…"},{"kind":"para","text":"4 Behold also the ships, which though they be so great, and are driven of fierce winds, yet are they turned about with a very small helm, whithersoever the governor listeth."},{"kind":"para","text":"It doesn’t take much for a ship to turn either. At least it shouldn’t. It’s probably the smallest part of the ship that is able to turn the whole thing. It’s a “small helm” as James puts it here. Why does it matter? Well, James gives a perfect illustration of these things, so we are able to apply it to ourselves in verse 5… 5 Even so the tongue is a little member, and boasteth great things. Behold, how great a matter a little fire kindleth! 6 And the tongue is a fire, a world of iniquity: so is the tongue among our members, that it defileth the whole body, and setteth on fire the course of nature; and it is set on fire of hell."},{"kind":"para","text":"A bad teacher, with a terrible tongue, can start a wild fire that cascades out of control simply because of one small lie or utterance. The tongue, in any human, is a powerful member. It can make or break a person."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Death and life are in the power of the tongue: and they that love it shall eat the fruit thereof.","ref":"Proverbs 18:21"},{"kind":"para","text":"Watching what we say is a very important factor to our faith and our growth. If we cannot learn to control our speech and minds, we will never grow in our faith and walk in the Lord. Even more so… You can tell a lot about other people’s faith based on their use of their speech as well. It’s a pretty major sign to believers when there are people who claim to know Christ, yet every other word that comes out of their mouth is foul, or using His name as a curse word."},{"kind":"para","text":"7 For every kind of beasts, and of birds, and of serpents, and of things in the sea, is tamed, and hath been tamed of mankind: 8 But the tongue can no man tame; it is an unruly evil, full of deadly poison."},{"kind":"para","text":"Interesting insight. You’d say, “But you just told us that we have to ‘control’ our speech and minds. How do we do that if James says no man can tame it?” The truth is, we are weak without Christ. Christ is a man, but He is also GOD. He is the only one that can help US tame our tongue. No one else can do that for us. We have to be obedient to His change in our speech, and His direction in how He would like us to talk if we are going to be able to control and tame our tongue."},{"kind":"para","text":"“Okay,” you say, “How do we know what is good and what is bad?” Great question! The Bible actually tells us!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let no man despise thy youth; but be thou an example of the believers, in word, in conversation, in charity, in spirit, in faith, in purity.","ref":"1 Timothy 4:12"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But as he which hath called you is holy, so be ye holy in all manner of conversation;","ref":"1 Peter 1:15"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"That ye put off concerning the former conversation the old man, which is corrupt according to the deceitful lusts;","ref":"Ephesians 4:22"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let no corrupt communication proceed out of your mouth, but that which is good to the use of edifying, that it may minister grace unto the hearers.","ref":"Ephesians 4:29"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let the words of my mouth, and the meditation of my heart, be acceptable in thy sight, O LORD, my strength, and my redeemer.","ref":"Psalm 19:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"The list could go on forever. The point is, we need to stop before we speak and ask ourselves, “Will this be bringing glory to the Lord if I say this? Will this point people to Him and His salvation?” If it does not, it’s best not to say it. 9 Therewith bless we God, even the Father; and therewith curse we men, which are made after the similitude of God. 10 Out of the same mouth proceedeth blessing and cursing. My brethren, these things ought not so to be."},{"kind":"para","text":"Our tongues have the ability to bring curses and blessings, and we must choose which we will provide to others. Without Christ, our tongues are untamed, and they are easily given to cursing. With Christ, we should love Him so much that we find every reason possible to provide blessings more than cursing. We cannot do both, though."},{"kind":"para","text":"A true barometer of our faith is indicated by whether we are able to speak more blessings or more cursings. If it seems we curse more than bless, then we have a failing faith (possibly no faith at all). If we are growing in our faith, we become more fruitful in our speech to bless (even blessing those who are extremely terrible or aggressive towards you)."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But I say unto you, Love your enemies, bless them that curse you, do good to them that hate you, and pray for them which despitefully use you, and persecute you;","ref":"Matthew 5:44"},{"kind":"para","text":"One or the other… James continues with a further illustration… 11 Doth a fountain send forth at the same place sweet water and bitter? 12 Can the fig tree, my brethren, bear olive berries? either a vine, figs? so can no fountain both yield salt water and fresh."},{"kind":"para","text":"We cannot be “Christians” and be bitter. We cannot be “Christians” and be olives and figs. We cannot be “Christians” and be salt and fresh water. We must be one or the other! If we are both, we are double-minded and two-faced. These are not good qualities to attribute to someone. No one likes to be called “two-faced.” Yet, this is who we are if we are claiming “Christ” yet displaying “Satan.” Remember what James said about this?"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"A double minded man is unstable in all his ways.","ref":"James 1:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"May we be cautious about our acclamation when we are living a life of hypocrisy. 13 Who is a wise man and endued with knowledge among you? let him shew out of a good conversation his works with meekness of wisdom."},{"kind":"para","text":"How do we know that what is being said from our brothers is true? How do we know if their faith is real, and their conduct is genuine? How do we know that they are not double-minded (maybe even playing the part), but inwardly they don’t know Christ? Look to their works."},{"kind":"para","text":"If they have the knowledge of God, they will continue in good works towards others. If they do not, and they are superficial on the outside, their good works will rot away. It will come to nothing without Christ. In fact, their good works are “no good.”"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But we are all as an unclean thing, and all our righteousnesses are as filthy rags; and we all do fade as a leaf; and our iniquities, like the wind, have taken us away.","ref":"Isaiah 64:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"Not only so, but the good works that people do without Christ will soon come to an end before too long. They look the part, they act the part, but it will all be found out. I know this from ministry experiences myself, but James also points this out in the next verse… 14 But if ye have bitter envying and strife in your hearts, glory not, and lie not against the truth. 15 This wisdom descendeth not from above, but is earthly, sensual, devilish. 16 For where envying and strife is, there is confusion and every evil work."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we are acting “good” and “godly” yet living in sin, bitterness, envy, and strife, we are ultimately lying against the truth. This means that the works we are doing are as James states “…earthly, sensual, devilish…” These things will bring about “envy and strife…confusion and every evil work.” It’s a dangerous thing to try and play the role of a follower of Christ and not actually be one. Though God can certainly use you, in the end, you will end up more empty than when you arrived."},{"kind":"para","text":"Many think that good works will bring them fulfillment, but as James pointed out numerous times already, nothing brings us fulfillment until we know Christ personally. Once we do, we will do God’s work out of a love for God, rather than out of a desire to be filled by Him. God has already filled those who trust in Him. In fact, if we are not filled by God, we have nothing to fill others with except our feeble selves and fleshly motives. This is a dangerous thing to give others because you are saying, “I want you to be more like me.” When we should be saying, “I want you to be more like Christ.” 17 But the wisdom that is from above is first pure, then peaceable, gentle, and easy to be intreated, full of mercy and good fruits, without partiality, and without hypocrisy."},{"kind":"para","text":"I’ve had many conversations about the differences between wisdom and knowledge. I won’t get into that in this setting, but I will say as a rule of thumb, and maybe as a means of simple understanding, wisdom is who Christ is, and knowledge is learning more about Him. When we come to know Christ, that is wisdom. When we become more like Him, that is knowledge."},{"kind":"para","text":"James says, wisdom (who is Christ) is first pure. There’s no sin, no wrong, no pain, no harm, no blur, no callousness, no strife, no envy, no confusion, no evil in God’s wisdom. Pure means there are no imperfections. God’s wisdom (found in Christ, of course) is first pure. We need to understand that there was no sin found in Christ. If we know God’s wisdom, we know Christ was pure!"},{"kind":"para","text":"Then it’s peaceable. It’s peaceable to the receiver and peaceable to those we give it to. In fact, God’s wisdom looks for peace."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"If it be possible, as much as lieth in you, live peaceably with all men.","ref":"Romans 12:18"},{"kind":"para","text":"God’s Wisdom (again, this is Christ) is peaceable!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And this shall be a sign unto you; Ye shall find the babe wrapped in swaddling clothes, lying in a manger. And suddenly there was with the angel a multitude of the heavenly host praising God, and saying, Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, good will toward men.","ref":"Luke 2:12-14"},{"kind":"para","text":"James points out that His wisdom is gentle. This is the embodiment of Christ. Everything Christ did was gentle. He did not go out of His way to make enemies on purpose. He did not try to beat the Bible into nonbelievers. He wanted to show gentleness and goodness to those He was trying to preach to. This is why His message was “easy to be intreated,” as the next characteristic implies."},{"kind":"para","text":"When the message of God is peaceable and gentle, it makes it much easier for others to take in. We have a lot of callous and cold preachers today who are looking to prove themselves right, instead of just letting God’s Word do the work. They try to take the place of the Holy Spirit and force conviction instead of just giving God’s wisdom, which is gentle and easy to be intreated. What they lack is mercy and good fruits, which is (ironically) the next two characteristics of God’s wisdom."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we want to just be right no matter what the cause is (even if we are right), we are not showing grace and mercy. The message becomes stale and bitter if we are just trying to be smarter than the other person instead of simply giving the truth to the one sitting across from you. We must be merciful, if not for the fact that God is continuously merciful to us."},{"kind":"para","text":"If we are able to do that, and we are able to walk in these other areas of God’s wisdom, we will have no choice but to produce “good fruits.” This means we will produce fruits of refreshment and nourishment. Fruits that will help people feel renewed and uplifted. We don’t have to “produce” these fruits in our works. They will automatically produce on their own if we are faithful to simply follow the Lord’s wisdom and leading. These are God’s fruit."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, longsuffering, gentleness, goodness, faith, Meekness, temperance: against such there is no law.","ref":"Galatians 5:22-23"},{"kind":"para","text":"Then James points out what everyone must come to understand. God’s wisdom doesn’t look for specific people to save. Certain people WILL be saved, yes, but that isn’t because God has closed off salvation for a certain group. Rather, He KNOWS who will accept Him and who won’t. His wisdom is “without” partiality. He treats everyone the same when it comes to salvation and the knowledge of it. In fact, the knowledge of salvation is so simple that even a child can understand it and be saved. This is intentional, so that there is no one in the world who can accuse God of making it too complicated."},{"kind":"para","text":"God does not complicate the salvation message. WE do. Churches complicate it. People add things to His message that are not written there. For instance, they might add a means of works in order for salvation to be accessible. God’s Word does not say we must work our way to Heaven. If it were that means, none of us would get there… Paul puts it this way."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I do not frustrate the grace of God: for if righteousness come by the law, then Christ is dead in vain.","ref":"Galatians 2:21"},{"kind":"para","text":"If we have to work our way to Heaven, then the purpose for Christ’s coming (living a perfect life and dying) would have been for nothing. Christ wasted His time. God put His power into the wrong person. God should have put His power into us. The thing is, the law just shows us what we CAN’T do. Christ shows us God’s wisdom, which is mercy and grace without partiality and (finally) without hypocrisy."},{"kind":"para","text":"God will never take back His salvation. He already completed it, and if He wanted to take it back, He can’t! Christ’s final words were “It is finished.” In other words, there’s nothing more that can be done; the debt is paid; there’s no other way to do it; and there’s no taking it back now. It’s complete. The final sacrifice was made."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"In whom ye also trusted, after that ye heard the word of truth, the gospel of your salvation: in whom also after that ye believed, ye were sealed with that holy Spirit of promise,","ref":"Ephesians 1:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul understood there was no taking back what Christ had done. God doesn’t want to take it back either. He did this on purpose. He wants us to be “sealed” by His Spirit for all of eternity. That was His ultimate goal."},{"kind":"para","text":"James understands it too (though he may have been skeptical of it his entire life until his brother rose from the grave three days later). The point is, James is penning down the difference between the world’s wisdom (working to make yourself feel better, and to gain kindness as well as a sense of purpose from others), versus God’s Wisdom (working because you love God and there is no way to pay back your debt to Him that He paid for you on the cross, and because you want others to know that same love you have from the Lord). This all culminates in this final verse of this chapter… 18 And the fruit of righteousness is sown in peace of them that make peace."},{"kind":"para","text":"You want to reap peace? You want to reap the benefits of your faith? You want to see the world change? You want to experience renewal in your life? Sow it! Sow peace into the world and you will reap it. Sow faith, and you will reap it. Someone once said, “The people that changed the world are the people whom the world couldn’t change.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Real lasting impact and reaping comes through valuable wisdom sowing. Sow the right wisdom, and reap the proper harvest. Sow the world’s wisdom, you will reap the world’s harvest. It might be an abundance with the world’s wisdom, but it will never last as long as an eternal harvest of Godly Wisdom."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2}],"verses":{"1":"My brethren, be not many masters, knowing that we shall receive the greater condemnation.","2":"For in many things we offend all. If any man offend not in word, the same is a perfect man, and able also to bridle the whole body."}},
    "4": {"chapter":4,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 4"},{"kind":"para","text":"Group: Saturday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter of James, we continue to go through a list of different tests that we will likely go through in this life to increase our faith. The chapter spoke specifically about being tested by our speech."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we see what happens when we fail these tests and how much of the world has provenly failed them. It's easy for us to reject the testing of God and then live a life that is pleasing to the world around us. It's easy for us to reject God altogether to try and gain worldly pleasure, and James will spend the majority of this chapter talking about why we should not do that..."}],"previously":["In our last chapter of James, we continued to go through a list of different tests that we will likely experience in this life to increase our faith. The chapter spoke specifically about being tested by our speech. In this chapter, we see what happens when we fail these tests and how much of the world has provenly failed them already and showed the results of it. It’s easy for us to reject the testing of God and then live a life that is pleasing to the world around us. It’s even easier for us to reject God altogether to try and gain worldly pleasure, and James will spend the majority of this chapter talking about why we should not do that…"],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":10,"title":"The Battle of Lust","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There’s a very large difference between wars and fightings. Wars are fightings, but on a much grander scale. Wars are usually out for blood, whereas fighting is usually out for ownership. When we fight, we fight to be right. When we war, we war to destroy the other people. The real issue behind both of these is actually one common trait which is “lust.” He continues… 2 Ye lust, and have not: ye kill, and desire to have, and cannot obtain: ye fight and war, yet ye have not, because ye ask not."},{"kind":"para","text":"“Lust” is a means to desire anything for personal gain over God. It doesn’t have to be explicitly sexual; you can lust after materialistic things as well. It’s important not to confuse lust with coveting. Coveting is wanting more than what you have, while lust is having more than what you should. The great result of lust is as James puts it, “You lust, and have not…” In other words, “You’ve spent all this time chasing after more and obtaining more, and yet it’s still not satisfying you.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"So what’s the next logical move? Take it out on others. This leads to coveting. James says, “You kill, and want more than what you have, yet it’s still never enough!” The culminating factor leading to “This could easily end, and there wouldn’t need to be fightings if you just asked…”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Ask who? Well, you could start by asking each other first. If that doesn’t prosper you, then ask the Lord. If that doesn’t prosper you, then don’t worry about it anymore. Of course, no one wants to wait on the Lord’s approval or denial, especially when it’s something we lust after. The Lord will say “no” because the things we desire most will get in the way of who we should desire most. God wants your full attention, and that’s why James says what he says next…"},{"kind":"para","text":"3 Ye ask, and receive not, because ye ask amiss, that ye may consume it upon your lusts."},{"kind":"para","text":"Again, this doesn’t have to be explicitly sexual, but I would say 80% of the lusts we desire are sexually charged. This includes homosexuality, transgenderism, and pedophilia today. These are the rising issues, and the reason that there is so much division in our country now."},{"kind":"para","text":"Could they ask for God’s permission in these areas? Absolutely! Have they asked for God’s permission in these areas? Of course not! Why? Because they know God is not okay with any of these things. Therefore, they chase their lusts, and cause fightings, and wars, and try and kill to obtain what they want, yet they are never satisfied and they never receive what they are longing for. They are looking in the wrong places! 4 Ye adulterers and adulteresses, know ye not that the friendship of the world is enmity with God? whosoever therefore will be a friend of the world is the enemy of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"They desire more from the world and less from the Lord. As of the time of this writing, there was a school shooting at a Presbyterian school in Tennessee. The attacker was a woman who identified as a man and pointedly attacked this school because of their Christian ethics (supposedly having attended this school when she was younger). Aside from the fact and obvious evidence that there was something wrong with her (who shoots and kills three 9-year-old kids?), she is the great picture of our current culture and James’ words of warning here. She wanted more than what God would allow her, and so she killed to get it (losing her own life in the process), and she still didn’t get what she truly wanted. Neither will she. There are others in her circle who feel sympathy for her and agreed with her motives, desiring more bloodshed."},{"kind":"para","text":"James says, “You can do whatever you want in this world to the people of God, and for personal profit, but you will never have God’s favor upon you.” In all honesty, it is far better to have God’s favor on you than to have the world’s riches. For with Christ, you are richer than anything the world can offer. 5 Do ye think that the scripture saith in vain, The spirit that dwelleth in us lusteth to envy?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Lust leads to envy every time. Envy means “jealousy.” It means we are jealous of what others have. The reason that others kill others is because they are jealous of what they have. America has been a target for much of the world for generations now, and it’s not because we are bad people (we stay quite secluded from battle, aside from aiding Ukraine right now, which is a whole different kind of conflict). No, the reason we are targeted so often is because we are prospering. Other nations lust after more, and so they envy us to try and steal and take what we have, or at the very least to diminish what we have so that we will have less than them."},{"kind":"para","text":"The 9/11 attacks are proof of this (as well as a whole list of others, including the American Revolution). Our short spirit desires temporal, mediocre things, when we should just be looking to what pleases the Lord. Honestly, if we pleased the Lord in everything we did, we could be the poorest people in wealth, but the richest people in Spirit. All of our needs would be taken care of, and we wouldn’t have to worry about the rest. 6 But he giveth more grace. Wherefore he saith, God resisteth the proud, but giveth grace unto the humble."},{"kind":"para","text":"This Bible verse should be marked in everyone’s Bible. You know what brings the Christian comfort during a conflict? God’s grace. God’s grace is everything we need. He rejects or “resists” those that reject Him. Those are the “proud.” But those that accept Him, and lean on Him, knowing how small and insignificant they are, those are the “humble.” Those are the ones that God gives His grace to."},{"kind":"para","text":"God’s grace is a very big deal, and it’s His grace that we should be striving towards in this life. There’s nothing more valuable in a human being’s life than God’s grace."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And if by grace, then is it no more of works: otherwise grace is no more grace. But if it be of works, then it is no more grace: otherwise work is no more work.","ref":"Romans 11:6"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let us therefore come boldly unto the throne of grace, that we may obtain mercy, and find grace to help in time of need.","ref":"Hebrews 4:16"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And he said unto me, My grace is sufficient for thee: for my strength is made perfect in weakness. Most gladly therefore will I rather glory in my infirmities, that the power of Christ may rest upon me.","ref":"2 Corinthians 12:9"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But none of these things move me, neither count I my life dear unto myself, so that I might finish my course with joy, and the ministry, which I have received of the Lord Jesus, to testify the gospel of the grace of God.","ref":"Acts 20:24"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the grace of God that bringeth salvation hath appeared to all men,","ref":"Titus 2:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"His grace is enough for you and sufficient for your needs. His grace never ends, and it is a source that we can continuously fill ourselves up with. His grace is the fire that builds our faith. It’s the ointment that heals our wounds. It’s the food that fills our spirits. It’s the rudder that steers our vessels. It’s the rock that never breaks. It’s the truth that never fails. It’s the sweetness to a bitter world. It’s the wheels that keep us moving. It’s the path that leads through the wilderness. It’s the stone that takes down the giant. The Burning Bush that never diminishes. The ark that brings us power. The water from a rock. The whale that takes us safely to shore. The oil that anoints our heads. The song that keeps us singing. The friend who sticks closer than a brother. The iron that sharpens us."},{"kind":"para","text":"It is the only thing a Christian needs in order to move in any direction in this life. His grace is all complete; it supplies every need. It’s what brings us “joy unspeakable and full of glory.” And it’s the only tool we have to overcome the temptations and trials of this world. 7 Submit yourselves therefore to God. Resist the devil, and he will flee from you."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s important that we don’t get this wrong. I’ve heard people say, “We are supposed to run from the devil, we need to stay away from what Satan is trying to do to us.” There are certainly times to run from sin, but there won’t be many opportunities to run from Satan. Satan is super subtle. You don’t expect him to be playing any tricks on you. Even Christians don’t expect it. We are often caught off guard."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, Satan will always do something that makes you question whether or not it’s biblically right to do it. He will entice you into thinking that it won’t be such a big deal to follow after this or do that. He will say, “It’s right for you to be bitter towards this person or that person. They mistreated you. God would want you to mark them, avoid them, and keep them at a distance.” When in actuality, we should be trying to find “peace with everyone” as much as we can."},{"kind":"para","text":"A red flag should go up when Satan tries to tempt you into ungodly things, no matter how small they may seem. When the red flag goes up, we should be seeking to resist the devil. If we resist him long enough and find the correct response to the issues, James tells us that Satan will have no choice but to leave."},{"kind":"para","text":"The term “flee” means “to escape safely out of danger.” When we are solidified on God’s Word and Will through the will and actions of Satan, it becomes dangerous territory for Satan, and he runs away. What a great promise made by James here through the Holy Spirit’s guidance. Furthermore, he gives clarification about what to do when it’s all said and done… 8 Draw nigh to God, and he will draw nigh to you. Cleanse your hands, ye sinners; and purify your hearts, ye double minded."},{"kind":"para","text":"It is ample to say that if drawing closer to God means He is drawing closer to us, then pulling away from God would mean He pulls away from us! James has already mentioned that God resists “the proud.” Is it possible for God to resist the saved if they are proud? I believe so. I believe that God has the right to reject His children if they are arrogant and boastful. They are going directly against His Spirit, and it’s sufficient for God to say, “Nope, I’m not going to bless you here, you have some fixing that needs to be done first.” The best way to draw close to God is to first repent of your errors and call on God to “cleanse your hands” and “purify your hearts.” The greatest show of repentance is this… 9 Be afflicted, and mourn, and weep: let your laughter be turned to mourning, and your joy to heaviness. 10 Humble yourselves in the sight of the Lord, and he shall lift you up."},{"kind":"para","text":"We cannot, and should not, come to the Lord with pridefulness, arrogance, or ill-will in our hearts. We should not approach His throne of grace thinking we are the best and that there’s nothing that needs to be changed. We must come to His throne, every time, with humility. We should ask Him, “What do I need to change to exemplify you better, Lord? What can I do differently? Help me to learn from you and avoid assuming I know it all. Even the small things, Lord, help me to relearn the small things.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"You might say, “Yeah, I know about God’s grace. That verse is nice and all, but I need more meat.” That proves you know nothing about God’s grace, and it’s time to get back to the basics. You might also say, “Yeah, I’m good at repentance, I don’t need to worry about verses like that.” That proves you need more repentance, and God must break your stony heart. You might say, “I’m not going through a trial, I don’t really care about what James is saying about testing and about wars and fighting because those don’t affect me.” That proves that God is going to put you through one because you have rejected His teaching on it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Humble yourselves in the sight of the Lord. This means we come to His Word, even in the small, minute issues that we think are not that important to us, and say, “Maybe there’s more here than I realize.” Even better than that, we can say, “Maybe I’m wrong about what I know on this topic.” Even better than that is, “Maybe I have a lot more to learn about this topic.” That’s the best sign of humility—being ready and anticipating that we don’t know enough on the issues we think we know the most about."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":12,"title":"Robbing God's Judgement","entries":[{"start":11,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"No one is above the law, and judging your brother puts yourself above the law, taking the judgment meant for the Lord away from Him. You try to take the position of God. This verse can be misconstrued into believing it is meant for sinners (those without Christ). No, it is meant for the children of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have an issue in our churches today of brothers in Christ judging and speaking evil of other brothers in Christ. It’s the most common issue I’ve seen in the church house (through four different churches I’ve been to). It’s the same issue in your church home today, I guarantee it. There are some in the church who don’t have many kind words to say about others. Maybe they display kindness in some fashion or form, but they then end up ruining their testimony the next time they open their mouth. They are more privy to criticizing than they are to loving their fellow brethren."},{"kind":"para","text":"I pray that it is not said of us, but this is certainly something we must all be cautious of, because it’s a very common trait today, and it’s one that most are not aware of in themselves. James is warning against this because when we do this, we take away from the Lord’s guidance and testimony. We reject the Lord just like the world rejects the Lord and goes after lust. Our lust, when we do this, is to lust after judgment and division."},{"kind":"para","text":"God is the judge, and when He judges, He does not do it to divide. He does it to add or multiply to our needs or to the people. That is the real lesson we must learn here today. When we are bitter towards someone and we speak evil about them, creating a judgment seat for ourselves, all we do is divide. When we judge, we divide. When God judges, He multiplies. We take life when we judge. God gives life when He judges. We diminish our faith when we judge. God increases our faith when He judges."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":17,"title":"Scheduling Priorities","entries":[{"start":13,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Listen, I’m not against making future plans and goals. However, we should not bank on those future plans and goals. Those future plans and goals are something we will strive towards today. What this is telling us is, if there is something we should be doing today, we should not be putting it off until tomorrow. We should take advantage of the time we have now, because we are not promised anymore time later. We aren’t even promised the time we have now. I could have a heart attack in the middle of me typing this out and be home with the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are many things in my calendar and to-do list that have not been done, but the biggest priorities (which are the people I talk to and need to contact, spend time with, or help with a certain need) have been taken care of right off the get-go of the day, because the other things on my calendar (though they are important) don’t need to be worried about if I were to die. If the people I love know that I love them, then I can die happy. If the work I need to do gets done, but the people never received my love, my death and life will be in vain."},{"kind":"para","text":"Don’t put off things that are important to get done with the smaller things that you think are more important. The best way to think about it is, “Does my work affect people more than my presence? If not, how can I prioritize my presence over my work?” Sometimes (like my wife) you may have to go to a building to do your work, and you have a set schedule for when you are supposed to do that. That makes it difficult to be in people’s presence. With modern technology, you are literally a text or phone call away from reaching out to someone and being present with them. Take the time to do that."},{"kind":"para","text":"If there is an opportunity for you to visit someone you need to visit right after you get off work, do that. Even if you do it alone, at least take the time to reach out and pour into people who value your faith. Ministering to people is the most needful work that this world craves, and it’s often the most overlooked because we are either too busy working or too busy checking off our to-dos. Whether that be working for our money or working for ourselves, we need to see the value of ministering to others’ needs over ministering for our own. This includes in our very own families in our own households."},{"kind":"para","text":"Our kids or spouses will be neglected more times than not because we are busy with this or that. This happens a lot in the household of pastors and preachers, believe it or not. They are so busy on their next sermon, event, or class material that they neglect the needs of their own family. It is better to minister to your family and not finish your notes for next Sunday than it is for you to finish your notes and miss out on the needs of your own family. 15 For that ye ought to say, If the Lord will, we shall live, and do this, or that."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is not a common phrase for many today. We think, “I’ll get this or that done.” We often tell the people at church, “See you next week!” We don’t know that. I’ve trained my brain to say, “Lord willing, I’ll see you in a few days!” I’ve even heard people say, “See you here, there, or in the air.” I personally like that saying, though it sounds quite cheesy. This is what James is reiterating. Our birthday was up to the Lord. Our death day will be up to the Lord. We don’t have a say in either case. This is great news for the Christian and very hard news for the nonbeliever. 16 But now ye rejoice in your boastings: all such rejoicing is evil. 17 Therefore to him that knoweth to do good, and doeth it not, to him it is sin."},{"kind":"para","text":"We cannot be so puffed up on ourselves and our own thoughts. We cannot think we are something more than we actually are. When we do, it is sin. When we put off the things we are supposed to do, knowing that it would be wiser for us to do it today, but we don’t do it today because we are lazy or think that it’s not that big of a deal (when the Spirit leads your heart, it’s a big deal every time), we are “knowing what is good,” and not doing it. That is sin."},{"kind":"para","text":"The bottom line is, we must take advantage of God’s leading today. We must do what pleases God, and we must have the proper knowledge of how to prioritize. I’ve heard ministers in the past say, “You cannot plan in ministry. You have to just be led by the Lord and go with it.” That is not true. You MOST CERTAINLY can plan your ministry, and it’s extremely wise to do so!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let all things be done decently and in order.","ref":"1 Corinthians 14:40"},{"kind":"para","text":"Everything that God did was in a specific order and purposely planned out. His second coming is also in a specific order and purposely planned out for a specific time. God has His own calendar of events, and He will fulfill what He has planned."},{"kind":"para","text":"The issue isn’t with the ability to plan; I believe there are a great deal of people that can do that. The issue is with prioritizing what you have planned, which is not a very easy thing to do. Do what’s good first! Do what’s needful second! Everything else you can get to when you have a chance. Otherwise, it’s sin."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":11,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":14}],"verses":{"1":"From whence come wars and fightings among you? come they not hence, even of your lusts that war in your members?","11":"Speak not evil one of another, brethren. He that speaketh evil of his brother, and judgeth his brother, speaketh evil of the law, and judgeth the law: but if thou judge the law, thou art not a doer of the law, but a judge.","12":"There is one lawgiver, who is able to save and to destroy: who art thou that judgest another?","13":"Go to now, ye that say, To day or to morrow we will go into such a city, and continue there a year, and buy and sell, and get gain:","14":"Whereas ye know not what shall be on the morrow. For what is your life? It is even a vapour, that appeareth for a little time, and then vanisheth away."}},
    "5": {"chapter":5,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 5"},{"kind":"para","text":"Group: Saturday"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: None"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, James has given us practical advice about what to look out for when we are tempted by \"lust,\" how to keep God on the throne for vengence and judgement, and keeping our priorities in order."},{"kind":"para","text":"James ends this letter off with a few warnings, and very useful application about how to reject envy and bitterness so we can be sucessful when we are faced with everyday life trials."}],"previously":["In our last chapter, James has given us practical advice about what to look out for when we are tempted by “lust,” how to keep God on the throne for vengeance and judgement, and keeping our priorities in order. James ends this letter off with a few warnings and very useful application about how to reject envy and bitterness."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"Warning the Rich","entries":[{"start":1,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Is this to say that having prosperity and being rich is a sin? Absolutely not. Solomon had plenty to be proud of in his wealth and privilege. In fact, he writes about what he has in Ecclesiastes."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I made me great works; I builded me houses; I planted me vineyards: I made me gardens and orchards, and I planted trees in them of all kind of fruits: I made me pools of water, to water therewith the wood that bringeth forth trees: I got me servants and maidens, and had servants born in my house; also I had great possessions of great and small cattle above all that were in Jerusalem before me: I gathered me also silver and gold, and the peculiar treasure of kings and of the provinces: I gat me men singers and women singers, and the delights of the sons of men, as musical instruments, and that of all sorts. So I was great, and increased more than all that were before me in Jerusalem: also my wisdom remained with me. And whatsoever mine eyes desired I kept not from them, I withheld not my heart from any joy; for my heart rejoiced in all my labour: and this was my portion of all my labour.","ref":"Ecclesiastes 2:4-10"},{"kind":"para","text":"If you go along this list, there’s nothing specifically sinful about it. He had everything in the world to help him prosper. God had blessed this man with more than necessary, and God blesses several people in the same way. The issue was not in the wealth of it all. The issue was in Solomon’s worship of it. He loved his riches more than his God."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then I looked on all the works that my hands had wrought, and on the labour that I had laboured to do: and, behold, all was vanity and vexation of spirit, and there was no profit under the sun.","ref":"Ecclesiastes 2:11"},{"kind":"para","text":"James is giving a measuring account of this same thing. Many people turn away from the Lord because their riches provide for them more than God does. They love their treasures more than they love the providence of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"Poor people, on the other hand, look for anything to give them riches. Because God is a gifter of great riches, poor people are more inclined to accept God’s riches. Rich people, however, don’t look for eternal riches because they think they have the best of the best right here on Earth. James wants it to be well known that their riches don’t matter. They will be taken, destroyed, and they will lose everything they have. Even if they didn’t lose it here and today, they will lose it for eternity. They will all be moth-eaten and destroyed."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Lay not up for yourselves treasures upon earth, where moth and rust doth corrupt, and where thieves break through and steal: But lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust doth corrupt, and where thieves do not break through nor steal: For where your treasure is, there will your heart be also.","ref":"Matthew 6:19-21"},{"kind":"para","text":"Where is your treasure? What are you investing in? Is it for an eternal cause, or for personal profit? If it’s for personal gain and worldly pleasures, James warns us what will eventually happen to us because of it. 3 Your gold and silver is cankered; and the rust of them shall be a witness against you, and shall eat your flesh as it were fire. Ye have heaped treasure together for the last days."},{"kind":"para","text":"Your profit and earthly riches will eat and abuse you. The more you have, the more miserable you will be. It doesn’t matter in the end, because the treasures of this world stay in this world, and God will try all things by fire, and those treasures will melt away, and nothing will remain. 4 Behold, the hire of the labourers who have reaped down your fields, which is of you kept back by fraud, crieth: and the cries of them which have reaped are entered into the ears of the Lord of sabaoth."},{"kind":"para","text":"James is accusing the rich of robbing from the poor to get to the top. The truth is, many rich people are only rich because they had to stand on others to get there. They stole from others, they mistreated others, they rejected others, they lied about others, and shamed others so that they can become superior!"},{"kind":"para","text":"I don’t know a single rich person who is not prideful. They are all proud of their position in this world, and they wear their pride on their sleeve. They like being the center of attention and they are happy at the fact that they had to destroy others’ lives to get to the position they are in. The truth is, God has specific plans for these people, and they will not enjoy their riches forever."},{"kind":"para","text":"They have a high now, and they may even have a high until they are old and fragile (maybe even to death), but God knows exactly what their rewards are in eternity. How sad and pathetic that there would be any on this earth who would reject God’s rewards, so they can benefit off of the poor of this world… 5 Ye have lived in pleasure on the earth, and been wanton; ye have nourished your hearts, as in a day of slaughter. 6 Ye have condemned and killed the just; and he doth not resist you."},{"kind":"para","text":"God has choice words for the rich of this world."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the disciples were astonished at his words. But Jesus answereth again, and saith unto them, Children, how hard is it for them that trust in riches to enter into the kingdom of God! It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle, than for a rich man to enter into the kingdom of God.","ref":"Mark 10:24-25"},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus even has an entire parable about the rich in this world…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"There was a certain rich man, which was clothed in purple and fine linen, and fared sumptuously every day: And there was a certain beggar named Lazarus, which was laid at his gate, full of sores, And desiring to be fed with the crumbs which fell from the rich man's table: moreover the dogs came and licked his sores. And it came to pass, that the beggar died, and was carried by the angels into Abraham's bosom: the rich man also died, and was buried; And in hell he lift up his eyes, being in torments, and seeth Abraham afar off, and Lazarus in his bosom. And he cried and said, Father Abraham, have mercy on me, and send Lazarus, that he may dip the tip of his finger in water, and cool my tongue; for I am tormented in this flame. But Abraham said, Son, remember that thou in thy lifetime receivedst thy good things, and likewise Lazarus evil things: but now he is comforted, and thou art tormented. And beside all this, between us and you there is a great gulf fixed: so that they which would pass from hence to you cannot; neither can they pass to us, that would come from thence. Then he said, I pray thee therefore, father, that thou wouldest send him to my father's house: For I have five brethren; that he may testify unto them, lest they also come into this place of torment. Abraham saith unto him, They have Moses and the prophets; let them hear them. And he said, Nay, father Abraham: but if one went unto them from the dead, they will repent. And he said unto him, If they hear not Moses and the prophets, neither will they be persuaded, though one rose from the dead.","ref":"Luke 16:19-31"},{"kind":"para","text":"The riches of this world, and the pride of being on top, blinds us from what really makes us rich…"}]}]},{"start":7,"end":11,"title":"Wait on God","entries":[{"start":7,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let the world have its riches. God tells His children to be rich as well! But we are rich in a different way! James has already spoken about this…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Every good gift and every perfect gift is from above, and cometh down from the Father of lights, with whom is no variableness, neither shadow of turning.","ref":"James 1:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"The gifts of God are worth more than any riches of this Earth! What are the rich gifts that God gives to His children? I can provide an extensive list, but I think a few will suffice. God gives His children blessings!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The blessing of the Lord, it maketh rich, and he addeth no sorrow with it.","ref":"Proverbs 10:22"},{"kind":"para","text":"What a relief to know that I never have to question my ethics. While the rich in this world sway on convictions, a rich Christian is happy to speak the truth and guide others into it. God also makes us rich in sustenance and providence!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But godliness with contentment is great gain. For we brought nothing into this world, and it is certain we can carry nothing out. And having food and raiment let us be therewith content. But they that will be rich fall into temptation and a snare, and into many foolish and hurtful lusts, which drown men in destruction and perdition.","ref":"1 Timothy 6:6-9"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul reminds us of what Jesus said. God makes us rich in food and shelter!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Therefore take no thought, saying, What shall we eat? or, What shall we drink? or, Wherewithal shall we be clothed? (For after all these things do the Gentiles seek:) for your heavenly Father knoweth that ye have need of all these things. But seek ye first the kingdom of God, and his righteousness; and all these things shall be added unto you.","ref":"Matthew 6:31-33"},{"kind":"para","text":"These all last for lifetimes. The rich have to work continuously to stay on top of their riches. The Christian has to just trust and be patient in the Lord to receive their riches. 9 Grudge not one against another, brethren, lest ye be condemned: behold, the judge standeth before the door."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is a very important lesson that James is teaching here, and it hits very close to home even still today. We see others being rich and making a name for themselves, and it’s enticing to us. Whom among us wouldn’t want to live a lifestyle that was so financially secure that we didn’t have to worry about food, our house payment, being able to put gas in our car, or even being able to OWN a car? Who wouldn’t want to be in a position where we can have everything just taken care of for us, and we don’t even have to go to the grocery store to buy our groceries for ourselves?"},{"kind":"para","text":"We see movie stars and world-famous musicians and think, “I wish I could have a life like that…” But in all reality, their riches are monetary and momentary. A Christian’s riches are not placed on their work. A Christian’s riches are placed on HIS work. There is no reason to be envious of the rich lifestyle when you have been given greater riches than they could ever fathom."},{"kind":"para","text":"Our job is not to envy them. It’s not to succumb to their authority or to their worldly thinking. Our job is not to hold grudges against anyone else, for God is the judge, and He will meet the needs of both His children and those that are not (whether through temporary pleasures or eternal profits, that is between you and the Lord). Our job is to suffer with patience. How do we do that? I’m glad you asked, because James answers that question… 10 Take, my brethren, the prophets, who have spoken in the name of the Lord, for an example of suffering affliction, and of patience. 11 Behold, we count them happy which endure. Ye have heard of the patience of Job, and have seen the end of the Lord; that the Lord is very pitiful, and of tender mercy."},{"kind":"para","text":"You didn’t get that promotion at work? Job lost his whole family, his income, and his housing all in one day. You don’t feel respected by your superiors? Jeremiah was thrown in prison by the rulers of his day for speaking the truth. You don’t think your boss likes you very much and is constantly trying to ruin your work ethic or life? Jesus was literally killed by His own people because He lived a Godly life."},{"kind":"para","text":"“What does any of these people’s afflictions have to do with anything? Job was miserable for years. Jeremiah felt hopeless and often hated his duties. Jesus purposely went to the cross because it meant saving everyone from their sins. I’m not these people,” you might say. James uses Job as the perfect illustration (we could use any of the prophets). Job was a man who experienced immense amounts of pain and lost so much of his riches."},{"kind":"para","text":"Job was an extremely rich man for his day. He may have even been the richest man in the world. God took care of him. He lost everything all in one day and then was plagued by gross, painful boils all over his body. He felt the Lord was punishing him. He was not happy about any of what God was doing to him to begin with because he felt God was against him. The story doesn’t end like that, though. The story ends with God triumphing, showing He is “pitiful, and of tender mercy.” And Job is found (after all of this has taken place) to be… happy!"},{"kind":"para","text":"Happiness does come if we continue to endure. As followers of Christ, we cannot give up when things are bleak and miserable. We must endure. There is a reward of love and happiness, and a gift of mercy and grace given to those who continue to endure the difficulties of the world. How do we endure? Well, we can’t run away. But furthermore, we have to simplify our situations."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"title":"Simplify the Problem","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Look how simple James makes it here. Endurance comes when we are able to simplify the problem. Yes’s are yes, and no’s are no. Don’t make any promises that are not worth making. Don’t assume. Don’t be critical. Don’t bother with the nonessentials. It’s okay to say yes to things that you know are important, and no to things you know will rob you of your joy. It’s that simple."},{"kind":"para","text":"Keeping Christ as our forefront and the perfect image and end goal makes these decisions 100x easier. The best way to start your trek of endurance, and to look for the end goal (keeping your eyes on the prize), is to know how to pray correctly…"}]}]},{"start":13,"end":20,"title":"How to Pray","entries":[{"start":13,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If you are afflicted, go to God. Give Him your burdens. Give Him your necessities. Give Him your care. Replace those burdens with prayer. If you are happy, sing to God. If you are sick, allow others to come and pray over your sickness, allow them to be afflicted with you. Bring every issue and every necessity before the Lord. 16 Confess your faults one to another, and pray one for another, that ye may be healed. The effectual fervent prayer of a righteous man availeth much."},{"kind":"para","text":"Confessing your faults does not mean the same thing as confessing your sins. The word “faults” here means “issues.” If we have done something wrong to others, we should confess those things we’ve done wrong. Even further, if we are going through different “issues,” we should talk to other believers about that. We confess our “sins” to God, and our “faults” to others. The more people we have praying for us, the more pleased our Father is to answer that prayer. God answers prayer no matter what. God LOVES answering prayer when it creates unity with His children."},{"kind":"para","text":"Even more so, if there is something hindering you (or hindering another Christian from their prayers being answered), there will be a child of God who can bring your burdens before the Lord, and God will respect the prayer of that one until the hinderance is removed from the others. 17 Elias was a man subject to like passions as we are, and he prayed earnestly that it might not rain: and it rained not on the earth by the space of three years and six months. 18 And he prayed again, and the heaven gave rain, and the earth brought forth her fruit."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s a great sign of faith-based prayer that is mentioned here. Elias (or Elisha) knew that his prayer would be answered in both instances. He had no hindrances between him and God, and God wanted to do a mighty work in Elias’ life and faith. Elias prayed two different times, and Elias’ prayer was answered both times. One for three years and six months, and the other prayer was immediate. This is genuine prayer and faith. This is what we should be relying on. Just pray for what you know needs to be done, and what you know is right ,and then let the Lord answer it how He pleases. Even a “no” is an answered prayer from the Lord. 19 Brethren, if any of you do err from the truth, and one convert him; 20 Let him know, that he which converteth the sinner from the error of his way shall save a soul from death, and shall hide a multitude of sins."},{"kind":"para","text":"Everything that James has spoken about through this Epistle has been solemnly based on applicability. We can take the advice from each chapter and actually use it to help increase our faith. It’s a letter on how to process pain in this world and what brings comfort to the Christian while everyone else seeks temporary relief. The application here, in this last part, is a tricky one."},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that James is speaking about a man who has not come to know Christ and is uninvolved with the truth. He says, “If there is someone who rejected the truth, yet a Christian is able to ‘convert’ him (into the truth) then the Christian that was able to help him go to the truth saved his soul from eternal death! And ALL of his sins (even his error of truth rejection) will be hidden (or forgiven)!”"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is how it works. We are lost, we are told how to be found, we are saved, and then our sins are forgiven. It’s really that simple. It’s so simple a child can understand it. It’s so simple, a rich man can understand it. It’s so simple, the worst human on Earth can understand it. That’s the business we should be in: converting sinners from the error of their ways and watching their lives change as Christ hides the multitudes of their sins."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":2},{"start":7,"end":8},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":15}],"verses":{"1":"Go to now, ye rich men, weep and howl for your miseries that shall come upon you.","2":"Your riches are corrupted, and your garments are motheaten.","7":"Be patient therefore, brethren, unto the coming of the Lord. Behold, the husbandman waiteth for the precious fruit of the earth, and hath long patience for it, until he receive the early and latter rain.","8":"Be ye also patient; stablish your hearts: for the coming of the Lord draweth nigh.","12":"But above all things, my brethren, swear not, neither by heaven, neither by the earth, neither by any other oath: but let your yea be yea; and your nay, nay; lest ye fall into condemnation.","13":"Is any among you afflicted? let him pray. Is any merry? let him sing psalms.","14":"Is any sick among you? let him call for the elders of the church; and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord:","15":"And the prayer of faith shall save the sick, and the Lord shall raise him up; and if he have committed sins, they shall be forgiven him."}},
  },
};
